1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,768
Somewhere in your Bible there's a short list of the ways God used to answer a king.

2
00:00:06,138 --> 00:00:08,889
By dreams, by the Urim, and by the prophets.

3
00:00:09,259 --> 00:00:14,182
And there's a night when none of them worked, for the same king who had driven every

4
00:00:14,182 --> 00:00:15,530
medium out of the land.

5
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:17,680
Think about who he was.

6
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:23,951
Saul was the first king Israel ever had, chosen by God and anointed by Samuel,

7
00:00:24,321 --> 00:00:26,738
a head taller than anyone in the nation.

8
00:00:27,108 --> 00:00:29,040
That night he broke his own law.

9
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:34,547
He put on borrowed clothes, slipped past the Philistine camp in the dark,

10
00:00:34,917 --> 00:00:37,595
and went looking for a woman who spoke with the dead.

11
00:00:37,965 --> 00:00:39,707
When she began, she screamed.

12
00:00:40,227 --> 00:00:43,340
She said she could see gods coming up out of the ground.

13
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:48,716
And the voice that answered Saul told him something that came true before the next

14
00:00:48,716 --> 00:00:49,143
sunset.

15
00:00:49,863 --> 00:00:55,031
In Sunday school the story of Saul usually ends with David and Goliath.

16
00:00:55,401 --> 00:00:57,855
Today we're going to read what comes after,

17
00:00:58,224 --> 00:01:02,550
because one of the most frightening nights in the Bible didn't happen on a

18
00:01:02,550 --> 00:01:03,251
battlefield.

19
00:01:03,622 --> 00:01:07,075
It happened in a small house in a village called Endor.

20
00:01:07,695 --> 00:01:12,861
If you like studying the Bible with the history beside it, subscribe to the channel.

21
00:01:12,861 --> 00:01:15,813
It helps a lot when you're just getting started.

22
00:01:16,184 --> 00:01:17,212
Let's begin.

23
00:01:17,931 --> 00:01:19,595
Chapter one. The request.

24
00:01:20,215 --> 00:01:23,952
Saul came from Benjamin, the smallest tribe in Israel,

25
00:01:24,322 --> 00:01:28,137
and his name in Hebrew is Sha'ul, which means asked for.

26
00:01:28,657 --> 00:01:30,320
That's how he got the throne.

27
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:35,858
The people looked at the nations around them and asked Samuel for a king like all

28
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:36,624
the nations.

29
00:01:36,995 --> 00:01:40,956
Hold on to that word, asked. It comes back at the very end.

30
00:01:41,475 --> 00:01:45,278
Samuel anointed him in private, pouring oil on his head.

31
00:01:45,648 --> 00:01:50,471
But on the day he was supposed to be presented to the whole nation, nobody could

32
00:01:50,471 --> 00:01:51,014
find him.

33
00:01:51,384 --> 00:01:54,631
The Bible says he had hidden himself among the baggage.

34
00:01:55,001 --> 00:01:59,834
When they finally pulled him out, the crowd saw what Samuel had seen: from his

35
00:01:59,834 --> 00:02:02,560
shoulders up he was taller than any of them.

36
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:07,417
And they shouted something that appears in the Bible for the first time right here.

37
00:02:07,787 --> 00:02:09,115
God save the king.

38
00:02:09,735 --> 00:02:14,670
People still say it three thousand years later, and very few know where it comes

39
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:14,978
from.

40
00:02:15,348 --> 00:02:17,531
His first real test came quickly.

41
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:21,632
An Ammonite king besieged a town called Jabesh-gilead

42
00:02:22,003 --> 00:02:27,208
and offered to make peace on one condition: he would put out the right eye of every

43
00:02:27,208 --> 00:02:28,211
man in the city.

44
00:02:28,581 --> 00:02:33,250
Saul raised an army overnight, attacked at dawn and broke the siege.

45
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:39,314
First Samuel eleven says that Saul and all the men of Israel rejoiced greatly.

46
00:02:39,684 --> 00:02:44,086
Keep Jabesh in mind, because that town never forgot what he did.

47
00:02:44,606 --> 00:02:49,039
If the story stopped there, Saul would be one of the heroes of the Bible.

48
00:02:49,409 --> 00:02:54,561
But the text keeps going, and in the middle of those victories it leaves warning

49
00:02:54,561 --> 00:02:56,750
signs for anyone who reads slowly.

50
00:02:57,370 --> 00:02:59,553
Chapter two. The three warnings.

51
00:03:00,173 --> 00:03:05,666
You can see them if you read First Samuel thirteen, fourteen and fifteen one after

52
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:06,336
the other.

53
00:03:06,706 --> 00:03:12,088
In chapter thirteen the Philistines are gathering, and Samuel tells Saul to wait

54
00:03:12,088 --> 00:03:13,366
seven days for him.

55
00:03:13,736 --> 00:03:19,272
On the seventh day the soldiers start slipping away, so Saul offers the sacrifice

56
00:03:19,272 --> 00:03:22,758
himself, which only the prophet was supposed to do.

57
00:03:23,127 --> 00:03:25,657
He has barely finished when Samuel arrives.

58
00:03:26,028 --> 00:03:29,038
Thou hast done foolishly, Samuel tells him.

59
00:03:29,408 --> 00:03:31,435
It was a matter of a few hours.

60
00:03:31,955 --> 00:03:37,452
In chapter fourteen, in the middle of a battle, Saul swears that any soldier who

61
00:03:37,452 --> 00:03:39,444
eats before evening will die.

62
00:03:39,814 --> 00:03:45,162
His son Jonathan never heard the oath and eats a little honey, and Saul is ready to

63
00:03:45,162 --> 00:03:46,257
put him to death.

64
00:03:46,626 --> 00:03:51,308
The army has to step in to save the man who had just won the battle for them.

65
00:03:51,679 --> 00:03:57,446
In chapter fifteen God sends Saul against Amalek with orders to destroy everything.

66
00:03:57,815 --> 00:04:01,657
Saul spares the king and keeps the best of the sheep and cattle.

67
00:04:02,028 --> 00:04:05,140
When Samuel confronts him, he says something odd.

68
00:04:05,510 --> 00:04:08,238
For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft.

69
00:04:08,857 --> 00:04:10,904
And yes, he really says witchcraft.

70
00:04:11,275 --> 00:04:14,701
Saul had nothing to do with anything like that at the time.

71
00:04:15,070 --> 00:04:20,425
Still, it's strange that this is the word Samuel picks on the day Saul begins to

72
00:04:20,425 --> 00:04:21,563
lose the kingdom.

73
00:04:22,083 --> 00:04:27,566
As Samuel turns to leave, Saul grabs the hem of his robe and it tears in his hand.

74
00:04:27,936 --> 00:04:30,384
Samuel looks at the torn cloth and says,

75
00:04:30,754 --> 00:04:34,290
The Lord hath rent the kingdom of Israel from thee this day.

76
00:04:34,661 --> 00:04:38,177
That robe comes back later, in a place you wouldn't expect.

77
00:04:38,796 --> 00:04:40,547
There was a human cost, too.

78
00:04:40,917 --> 00:04:44,264
Once David became popular, Saul turned on him,

79
00:04:44,634 --> 00:04:49,652
and when the priests of a town called Nob gave David bread and a sword, Saul had

80
00:04:49,652 --> 00:04:50,405
them killed.

81
00:04:50,775 --> 00:04:52,819
Eighty-five priests died that day.

82
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:59,562
Only one escaped, a young priest named Abiathar, and he ran to David carrying the

83
00:04:59,562 --> 00:05:00,015
ephod,

84
00:05:00,384 --> 00:05:04,793
the priestly vest that was used to ask God questions through the Urim.

85
00:05:05,163 --> 00:05:10,113
Saul's fear had a price, and the priesthood paid it first. He would pay for it

86
00:05:10,113 --> 00:05:10,494
later.

87
00:05:11,114 --> 00:05:16,383
Do you want more studies like this, ones that read the whole story without skipping

88
00:05:16,383 --> 00:05:21,398
the hard parts? Tell me in the comments which Bible character you'd like to see

89
00:05:21,398 --> 00:05:21,715
next.

90
00:05:22,336 --> 00:05:24,154
Chapter three. The silence.

91
00:05:24,774 --> 00:05:30,108
That brings us to chapter twenty eight, which opens with two facts placed side by

92
00:05:30,108 --> 00:05:30,437
side.

93
00:05:30,807 --> 00:05:34,769
Now Samuel was dead, and all Israel had lamented him.

94
00:05:35,139 --> 00:05:40,542
And Saul had put away those that had familiar spirits, and the wizards, out of the

95
00:05:40,542 --> 00:05:40,871
land.

96
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:46,585
The prophet who made him king was gone, and the mediums were gone too, because Saul

97
00:05:46,585 --> 00:05:48,259
himself had banished them.

98
00:05:48,628 --> 00:05:52,617
Then the Philistines gathered their whole army and camped at Shunem,

99
00:05:52,987 --> 00:05:57,174
and Saul set up his lines across the valley on Mount Gilboa.

100
00:05:57,544 --> 00:06:00,403
The text is very direct about what he felt.

101
00:06:00,773 --> 00:06:03,758
He was afraid, and his heart greatly trembled.

102
00:06:04,127 --> 00:06:06,734
So he asked God, and nothing came back.

103
00:06:07,104 --> 00:06:11,306
No dream, no answer from the Urim, no word from any prophet.

104
00:06:11,676 --> 00:06:17,083
The Lord answered him not, neither by dreams, nor by Urim, nor by prophets.

105
00:06:17,803 --> 00:06:23,028
Look at the words answered not. Every channel God normally used was closed.

106
00:06:23,398 --> 00:06:28,698
And as for the Urim, the priest who carried the ephod had been in David's camp for

107
00:06:28,698 --> 00:06:29,086
years.

108
00:06:29,606 --> 00:06:32,379
A fall like this doesn't happen overnight.

109
00:06:32,749 --> 00:06:36,471
Saul didn't wake up one morning wanting to consult the dead.

110
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:42,415
It started with a sacrifice made out of impatience, then an oath made out of pride,

111
00:06:42,415 --> 00:06:45,707
then the spoil he kept and the priests he killed.

112
00:06:46,078 --> 00:06:50,918
Each step seemed reasonable at the time, and together they led him to Endor.

113
00:06:51,538 --> 00:06:53,045
Then comes verse seven.

114
00:06:53,415 --> 00:06:56,001
Seek me a woman that hath a familiar spirit.

115
00:06:56,370 --> 00:06:59,276
The Hebrew calls her a mistress of the 'ov.

116
00:06:59,646 --> 00:07:04,850
Many scholars link that word to a ritual pit in the ground, an opening through which

117
00:07:04,850 --> 00:07:07,142
people believed the dead could speak.

118
00:07:07,512 --> 00:07:10,428
And his servants knew exactly where to find one.

119
00:07:10,798 --> 00:07:13,875
There is a woman that hath a familiar spirit at Endor.

120
00:07:14,495 --> 00:07:16,226
Chapter four. The scream.

121
00:07:16,846 --> 00:07:18,524
It helps to look at a map here.

122
00:07:18,894 --> 00:07:23,386
Endor sits on the far side of the hill where the Philistines were camped.

123
00:07:23,756 --> 00:07:29,223
To reach it, the king of Israel had to go around the enemy army, at night, a few

124
00:07:29,223 --> 00:07:30,863
hours before the battle.

125
00:07:31,383 --> 00:07:34,804
And Saul disguised himself, and put on other raiment.

126
00:07:35,173 --> 00:07:39,223
He took off what made him king and went as an ordinary man.

127
00:07:39,644 --> 00:07:41,819
The woman was suspicious from the start.

128
00:07:42,189 --> 00:07:47,373
You know what Saul has done. He has cut off the mediums from the land. Why are you

129
00:07:47,373 --> 00:07:49,017
laying a trap for my life?

130
00:07:49,387 --> 00:07:51,340
And Saul answered her with an oath.

131
00:07:51,710 --> 00:07:56,114
As the Lord liveth, there shall no punishment happen to thee for this thing.

132
00:07:56,484 --> 00:08:01,837
Years earlier he had expelled the mediums in the name of the Lord. Now he was using

133
00:08:01,837 --> 00:08:03,772
that same name to protect one.

134
00:08:04,142 --> 00:08:09,650
What Samuel had once said as a comparison, that rebellion is like witchcraft, had

135
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:12,098
become a place Saul was standing in.

136
00:08:12,718 --> 00:08:14,658
Whom shall I bring up unto thee?

137
00:08:15,078 --> 00:08:16,340
Bring me up Samuel.

138
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:21,254
When the woman saw Samuel, she cried out with a loud voice.

139
00:08:21,624 --> 00:08:26,243
This was her trade, and yet when it actually happened she was terrified.

140
00:08:26,613 --> 00:08:29,973
In the same moment she realized who her visitor was.

141
00:08:30,343 --> 00:08:33,591
Why hast thou deceived me? for thou art Saul.

142
00:08:34,111 --> 00:08:39,496
Saul asked her what she saw, and she gave one of the strangest answers in the Bible.

143
00:08:39,866 --> 00:08:42,181
I saw gods ascending out of the earth.

144
00:08:42,801 --> 00:08:49,220
The word is elohim, the same word used for God, for angels and for spiritual beings,

145
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:52,276
and she saw it rising out of the ground.

146
00:08:52,646 --> 00:08:54,076
What form is he of?

147
00:08:54,496 --> 00:08:58,061
An old man cometh up; and he is covered with a mantle.

148
00:08:58,682 --> 00:08:59,786
That's the robe.

149
00:09:00,206 --> 00:09:06,337
In Hebrew it's the same word, me'il, used for the robe Saul tore in chapter fifteen,

150
00:09:06,707 --> 00:09:11,944
and for the little robe Samuel's mother made for him every year when he was a boy

151
00:09:11,944 --> 00:09:13,625
serving at the tabernacle.

152
00:09:13,995 --> 00:09:15,891
That's how Saul knew it was Samuel,

153
00:09:16,261 --> 00:09:18,684
and he bowed with his face to the ground.

154
00:09:19,404 --> 00:09:21,160
Chapter five. Tomorrow.

155
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:26,280
The figure spoke first, and he sounded like a man who had been disturbed.

156
00:09:26,650 --> 00:09:29,544
Why hast thou disquieted me, to bring me up?

157
00:09:29,914 --> 00:09:31,577
Saul told him everything.

158
00:09:31,947 --> 00:09:37,821
The Philistines make war against me, and God is departed from me, and answereth me

159
00:09:37,821 --> 00:09:38,394
no more.

160
00:09:38,764 --> 00:09:40,485
The reply gave him nothing.

161
00:09:40,855 --> 00:09:45,774
Wherefore then dost thou ask of me, seeing the Lord is departed from thee?

162
00:09:46,294 --> 00:09:51,543
Saul had crossed enemy lines hoping to hear something new, and he heard the same

163
00:09:51,543 --> 00:09:54,299
message Samuel had given him years before:

164
00:09:54,669 --> 00:10:00,054
the kingdom would be torn from him and given to David, because of what happened with

165
00:10:00,054 --> 00:10:00,503
Amalek.

166
00:10:00,872 --> 00:10:03,328
Then came the one part he had never heard.

167
00:10:03,849 --> 00:10:06,647
To-morrow shalt thou and thy sons be with me.

168
00:10:07,766 --> 00:10:13,069
This was the king who had killed the priests of Nob, and the last prophecy he ever

169
00:10:13,069 --> 00:10:16,368
heard reached him in the dark, in a medium's house.

170
00:10:16,889 --> 00:10:21,592
Saul fell full length on the floor. He hadn't eaten all day or all night.

171
00:10:21,962 --> 00:10:24,402
What the woman did next is easy to miss.

172
00:10:24,772 --> 00:10:29,216
She killed the calf she had, baked bread, and set a meal in front of him.

173
00:10:29,586 --> 00:10:34,388
The last meal anyone ever served Saul came from a woman he had outlawed.

174
00:10:34,757 --> 00:10:37,700
Then he and his men went back out into the night.

175
00:10:38,421 --> 00:10:42,604
The next day on Mount Gilboa it happened just as he had been told.

176
00:10:42,974 --> 00:10:46,268
Three of his sons were killed, Jonathan among them.

177
00:10:46,637 --> 00:10:52,403
Saul was badly wounded by archers, begged his armour bearer to finish him, and when

178
00:10:52,403 --> 00:10:55,320
the man refused, he fell on his own sword.

179
00:10:56,040 --> 00:11:00,851
The Philistines took his armour and placed it in the temple of Ashtaroth,

180
00:11:01,221 --> 00:11:05,261
and they fastened his body to the wall of a city called Beth-shan.

181
00:11:05,631 --> 00:11:10,138
The night before, he had taken off his royal clothes to go to Endor.

182
00:11:10,507 --> 00:11:15,419
By the next evening his armour was hanging in a pagan temple as a trophy.

183
00:11:16,039 --> 00:11:20,358
This is one of the places where the story touches archaeology.

184
00:11:20,728 --> 00:11:26,214
Beth-shan is today's Tel Beit She'an, one of the most excavated sites in Israel.

185
00:11:26,584 --> 00:11:31,461
Archaeologists found temples there from roughly the right period, and some have

186
00:11:31,461 --> 00:11:33,807
tried to connect them to this chapter,

187
00:11:34,177 --> 00:11:39,449
while others think the link is too uncertain. That debate is still open, and it's

188
00:11:39,449 --> 00:11:40,425
fair to say so.

189
00:11:40,795 --> 00:11:43,292
Either way, this happened in a real place.

190
00:11:43,912 --> 00:11:45,743
The story has one more turn.

191
00:11:46,113 --> 00:11:50,949
When the men of Jabesh-gilead heard what had been done to Saul, they walked all

192
00:11:50,949 --> 00:11:51,316
night,

193
00:11:51,687 --> 00:11:57,022
took his body and his sons' bodies down from the wall, and buried them under a tree.

194
00:11:57,391 --> 00:12:02,191
The first town he ever rescued was the last one that stayed loyal to him.

195
00:12:03,011 --> 00:12:04,767
Chapter six. Who came up?

196
00:12:05,387 --> 00:12:10,179
Centuries later the book of Chronicles summed up his life in two verses.

197
00:12:10,549 --> 00:12:16,404
Saul died for asking counsel of one that had a familiar spirit, and enquired not of

198
00:12:16,404 --> 00:12:17,039
the Lord.

199
00:12:17,409 --> 00:12:21,420
In Hebrew, that word asking comes from the same root as his name.

200
00:12:21,790 --> 00:12:26,159
Sha'ul, the king the people asked for, died asking the wrong source.

201
00:12:26,979 --> 00:12:31,461
That still leaves the hardest question: who actually came up at Endor?

202
00:12:31,981 --> 00:12:37,117
Christians have disagreed about this for nearly two thousand years, so it's worth

203
00:12:37,117 --> 00:12:39,210
laying out the main views fairly.

204
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:41,181
Some think the woman faked it.

205
00:12:41,551 --> 00:12:46,763
The problem is that she screamed, which isn't how someone reacts to her own act, and

206
00:12:46,763 --> 00:12:50,300
she had no way of knowing what would happen the next day.

207
00:12:50,670 --> 00:12:54,813
Others believe it was an evil spirit impersonating Samuel.

208
00:12:55,184 --> 00:12:58,406
Tertullian argued this around the year two hundred.

209
00:12:58,776 --> 00:13:04,143
God had forbidden this practice, Ecclesiastes says that the dead know not any thing,

210
00:13:04,512 --> 00:13:09,763
the figure came up from below, and he told a rebellious king that they would soon be

211
00:13:09,763 --> 00:13:10,326
together.

212
00:13:10,846 --> 00:13:16,018
Others say it really was Samuel, sent by God rather than summoned by the woman.

213
00:13:16,388 --> 00:13:21,221
Justin Martyr thought so, and Origen preached a whole sermon defending it.

214
00:13:21,591 --> 00:13:26,878
The ancient Jewish book of Sirach says that Samuel after his death prophesied, and

215
00:13:26,878 --> 00:13:28,425
shewed the king his end.

216
00:13:28,795 --> 00:13:33,999
The text itself also keeps calling him Samuel and never hints at an impostor.

217
00:13:34,619 --> 00:13:36,957
The Bible doesn't tell us how it happened.

218
00:13:37,328 --> 00:13:42,725
What all three views have to admit is that the woman wasn't in control, the message

219
00:13:42,725 --> 00:13:44,611
wasn't new, and it came true.

220
00:13:44,980 --> 00:13:50,586
If it was Samuel, he said nothing God hadn't already said. If it was something else,

221
00:13:50,586 --> 00:13:52,855
even that had to repeat the truth.

222
00:13:53,225 --> 00:13:58,109
Saul went to Endor looking for a way out, and all he found was the answer he had

223
00:13:58,109 --> 00:13:59,208
been running from.

224
00:14:00,128 --> 00:14:02,601
So what do we take from a chapter like this?

225
00:14:03,221 --> 00:14:08,594
First, God's silence isn't permission to go looking somewhere else. Saul treated it

226
00:14:08,594 --> 00:14:11,895
like a locked door and went searching for a window.

227
00:14:12,264 --> 00:14:18,511
Second, the falls that end badly usually start small: a little impatience, a promise

228
00:14:18,511 --> 00:14:21,263
made in pride, a few sheep kept back.

229
00:14:21,634 --> 00:14:26,881
Third, the cost doesn't always show up right away. Saul banished the mediums and

230
00:14:26,881 --> 00:14:32,062
killed the priests years before that night, and it all came due in a few hours.

231
00:14:32,432 --> 00:14:37,106
And fourth, the dead had nothing new to tell him. Isaiah put it this way:

232
00:14:37,475 --> 00:14:41,631
Should not a people seek unto their God? for the living to the dead?

233
00:14:42,351 --> 00:14:43,943
There's one last irony.

234
00:14:44,313 --> 00:14:49,546
When the news reached David, the man Saul had chased for years, he wrote a lament

235
00:14:49,546 --> 00:14:50,063
for him,

236
00:14:50,433 --> 00:14:53,453
and it contains the line How are the mighty fallen!

237
00:14:53,823 --> 00:14:58,421
The person Saul tried hardest to kill was the one who mourned him best.

238
00:14:59,141 --> 00:15:04,323
The question was never really whether the dead can speak. It was whose voice Saul

239
00:15:04,323 --> 00:15:05,923
had stopped listening to.

240
00:15:06,843 --> 00:15:09,644
We don't need a pit in the ground to hear from God.

241
00:15:10,014 --> 00:15:15,498
Jesus told a story about a rich man who begged for someone to go back from the dead

242
00:15:15,498 --> 00:15:18,075
and warn his brothers, and he was told:

243
00:15:18,444 --> 00:15:23,815
If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one

244
00:15:23,815 --> 00:15:25,045
rose from the dead.

245
00:15:25,665 --> 00:15:30,504
Someone did rise. He didn't come up out of the earth; He walked out of a tomb.

246
00:15:30,874 --> 00:15:36,095
And to the dying man beside Him, He didn't say tomorrow you will be with me as a

247
00:15:36,095 --> 00:15:36,682
judgment.

248
00:15:37,052 --> 00:15:40,368
He said, Today shalt thou be with me in paradise.

249
00:15:41,288 --> 00:15:46,034
If you want to take this further, read First Samuel fifteen and twenty eight

250
00:15:46,034 --> 00:15:48,469
together, then the end of Luke sixteen,

251
00:15:48,838 --> 00:15:53,269
and ask yourself where you go looking for answers when God seems quiet.

252
00:15:53,989 --> 00:15:58,144
If this study helped you, subscribe and turn on notifications.

253
00:15:58,515 --> 00:16:03,986
In the next video we'll read another chapter most people skip, about a man the Bible

254
00:16:03,986 --> 00:16:09,456
lists among the heroes of faith and the promise he made concerning his own daughter.

255
00:16:09,826 --> 00:16:11,628
It's darker than you might think.
